This is a common React gotcha. If your useEffect is firing on every render despite having a dependency array, the most likely culprit is that one of the dependencies is being created fresh on every render — like an inline object, function, or array. For example:

```javascript
// ❌ Problem: This object is new on every render
useEffect(() => {
  console.log('re-rendering!');
}, [{}]); // {} is never equal to {}, so this runs every time

// ✅ Fix: Move the dependency outside
const deps = {};
useEffect(() => {
  console.log('re-rendering!');
}, [deps]);
```

The same happens with inline functions:
```javascript
// ❌ Problem: The function reference is new every time
useEffect(() => { /* ... */ }, [() => { /* ... */ }]);

// ✅ Fix: Define the function outside
const handler = () => { /* ... */ };
useEffect(() => { /* ... */ }, [handler]);
```

